{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/stamped-research-klyewiss/skills/stamped-conventions/references/principles-card.md; cat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/stamped-research-klyewiss/skills/stamped-conventions/references/project-kinds.md; rg --files /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/stamped-research-klyewiss/skills/stamped-conventions/references/stacks'","aggregated_output":"# STAMPED in one screen\n\nNormative text lives in the [paper](https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-paper) and in [stamped-principles-schema](https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-principles-schema).\nThis card exists so a session can hold the shape of STAMPED without loading either, and so no skill retypes them.\nWhen a judgement turns on wording, open the paper rather than this card.\n\n**A STAMPED research object is one versioned boundary that carries everything needed to re-run its own results.**\n\n| | Principle | Codes in [principles 0.2.0](https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-principles-schema/blob/v0.2.0/stamped-principles.json) | The question it answers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **S** | Self-contained | S.1 (MUST) | Is everything essential reachable under one top-level root? |\n| **T** | Tracked | T.1, T.3, T.5 (MUST) · T.2, T.4 (SHOULD) | Is every component content-identified, is every change recorded, and do run records name the versions involved? |\n| **A** | Actionable | A.1 (MUST) · A.2 (SHOULD) | Can a reader reproduce the results from the instructions, and are the procedures executable rather than prose? |\n| **M** | Modular | M.1, M.4 (SHOULD) · M.2 (MAY) | Are raw data, derivatives, code and environment separable, recombinable, and separated by access requirements? |\n| **P** | Portable | P.1, P.2, P.3 (MUST) | Does it avoid undocumented host state, specify the environment explicitly, and version-control that specification? |\n| **E** | Ephemeral | E.1 (SHOULD) | Are results produced in disposable environments, one per execution? |\n| **D** | Distributable | D.1 (MUST) · D.2 (SHOULD) | Can others persistently retrieve every referenced component, and rebuild the environment from what is published? |\n\n## What to say instead of restating\n\n- The **principles** are the 17 statements above; quote at most the one code under discussion.\n- The **checklist** is 31 questions derived from them (17 MUST, 9 SHOULD, 5 MAY), pinned at 0.3.1 in `../checklist/stamped-checklist-0.3.1.json` — read ids and text from there.\n- The **web app** at <https://checklist.stamped-principles.org/> is where a person fills or reads a checklist.\n- STAMPED **generalises YODA**: a YODA-shaped DataLad dataset is one way to satisfy it, not the definition. Nothing in these skills is YODA-specific.\n\n## Known drift to be aware of (and not to paper over)\n\n- The paper names **S.2**, **M.3** and **D.3** — all three about licensing; principles-schema 0.2.0 does not carry them, so none of the 31 checklist items asks about a licence.\n  The paper lists 19 principles, the schema 17 ([`docs/stamped/upstream/paper-schema-drift-issue.md`](https://github.com/con/skills/blob/master/docs/stamped/upstream/paper-schema-drift-issue.md) in con/skills has the line numbers; it is not part of an installed copy of this skill).\n  The assessor reports licence findings as an extra item outside the checklist rather than bending an existing id ([`response-format.md`](response-format.md)).\n- **T.4** (\"code-driven provenance SHOULD be recorded programmatically\") exists in the schema but has **no checklist item**.\n  Evidence for it is reported under the T items it supports, with the gap named.\n  The paper's T.4 is one compound statement; the schema splits it into T.4 (SHOULD, programmatic) and **T.5** (MUST, versions recorded), so T.5 has no paper counterpart to cite.\n- **must/014 and should/008 are the same sentence**, word for word: \"Is there a process for updating environment specifications when dependencies change, and are these updates tracked in version control?\" — one as a MUST under P.3, one as a SHOULD under D.2.\n  Answer both, from the same evidence, and say in the report that they are duplicates rather than letting a reader think two different things were checked.\n  Nothing in the checklist explains the duplication; it is in the upstream issue draft.\n- @asmacdo's draft in [stamped-agent-skills](https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-agent-skills) cites \"checklist v1.0.1\"; that is the web app's `package.json` version.\n  The checklist's own `checklist_version` is **0.3.1**.\n# Project kinds and emphasis\n\nSettle the kind **before** judging the object, from its documentation and the user's answers, not from a guess.\nAsk when it is not plain; a wrong kind moves the emphasis and produces a confidently wrong report.\nThe kind is recorded in the response JSON (`object.kind`) and, for eval runs, as the `rubric`.\n\n| Kind | What it is | Emphasis | Typically `not-applicable` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **dataset** | Data as the product: raw, derivative, or both | **M**odularity (raw vs derived vs code), **P**ortability of whatever produced it, **D**istributability (retrievable by others, licence stated) | E.1 only if the data was not computed at all (acquired raw data) |\n| **component / tool** | Code others call: a library, a container recipe, a workflow | **E**phemerality (each execution in a disposable environment), **P**ortability, **A**ctionability (tests and an entry point) | Nothing by default; a tool with no results of its own answers must/006 about its own test runs |\n| **study** | A question answered with data and compute: the usual shape of these skills' output | All seven; **T** (results linked to actions and environments) and **S** (essential inputs included or retrievably linked within the declared boundary) carry the most weight | Rarely anything |\n| **skill / agent harness** | A skill, prompt collection or agent configuration — including the skills in this collection | **T** (`version:`, changelog), **A** (tests outside the skill directory), **P** (no host assumptions), **D** (installable at a pin) | may/001 where there are no external modules |\n\n## A shape that comes up constantly\n\n**A study whose product is a re-derivation from another dataset's derivatives** — it consumes published results, adds a join, a table and a figure, and produces no raw or intermediate data of its own.\nIt is a **study**, not a dataset: the product is an answer, and the inputs belong to someone else.\nWhat follows: S.1 and M.1 are judged on identifiable inputs and component boundaries, using the equivalent-evidence interpretations in [`verdicts.md`](verdicts.md); T is judged on the few derived files it does make, and \"few\" is not a discount; E.1 is weighed lightly if the computation is seconds of CPU, and never counted against the object for being cheap.\n\n## Mixed objects\n\nMost real projects are more than one kind.\nName the **primary** kind, list the secondary ones, and judge each item against the component it applies to — a study that ships a reusable converter is judged on E.1 for the converter and on T for the results.\nSay in the report which component each verdict was read from.\n\n## What the emphasis changes, and what it does not\n\n- It changes **what to fix first** (report order), and the \"why it matters\" sentence attached to each fix.\n- It changes which items are honestly `not-applicable`.\n- It does **not** change a MUST into a SHOULD, and it never lowers the evidence bar.\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-ai-research-next/stamped-research-klyewiss/skills/stamped-conventions/references/stacks/datalad.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
